\details{
This function calculates all pairwise overlap counts for
a list of peak sets.

Note that the number of overlapping peaks is not
always symmetric, for example 100 peaks in \code{SetA}
might overlap 75 peaks in \code{SetB}.

The function can optionally filter peaks using \code{min_score},
which is applied to each set of peaks in \code{grl}. Note that
\code{min_score} can be a vector with length \code{length(grl)}
so that each peak set can be filtered using its own
distinct score.

The argument \code{metric} defines the type of overlap to
calculate. Each method can have the suffix \verb{_bases}
which means the calculation is based upon the number
of base positions, and not the number of peak regions.
By default all calculations are performed and returned,
however a subset can be provided which may be slightly
faster to calculate.

A brief description of terms used in the calculations:
\itemize{
\item \code{num_A}: number of peaks in A
\item \code{num_B}: number of peaks in B
\item \code{num_A_over_B}: number of peaks in A that overlap any peak in B
\item \code{bases_A}: number of unique genome bases covered by peaks in A
\item \code{bases_B}: number of unique genome bases covered by peaks in B
\item \code{bases_A_over_B}: number of bases where peaks in A overlap peaks in B
}

A brief description of each metric:
\itemize{
\item \code{count}: num_A_overB
\item \code{percent}: num_A_overB / num_A
\item \code{maxpercent}: num_A_overB / minimum( num_A, num_B )
\item \code{jaccard}: num_A_overB / ( num_A + num_B - num_A_over_B )
\item \code{count_bases}: bases_A_overB
\item \code{percent_bases}: bases_A_overB / bases_A
\item \code{maxpercent_bases}: bases_A_overB / minimum( bases_A, bases_B )
\item \code{jaccard_bases}: bases_A_overB / ( bases_A + bases_B - bases_A_over_B )
}

The argument \code{count_method} is relevant only to peak-based
overlaps, not for the metric \verb{_bases}. It defines whether
to return: the number of peaks in A that overlap peaks
in B, regardless whether any one peak is overlapped
multiple times; or return the total number of overlap
events even including peaks with multiple overlaps.
In practice the different is usually quite small, and
is relevant usually only when the peak sizes between
A and B are quite different, or include large regions
of maybe 1,000 base width or larger. In these cases,
it may be more useful and informative to use the \verb{_bases}
metric calculations.

Note that this method has not been tested extensively
for strand-specific overlaps, using \code{ignore.strand=FALSE},
although the calculations metrics should be applicable.
One might use strand-specific overlaps only with
RNA-seq protocols that are strand-specific, for example
to test the number of overlaps with annotated stranded
transcript features.

For several of the calculations, the possibility of zero
denominator is prevented by forcing minimum value \code{1},
which would render the result \code{0} in that case. This
result is considered more consistent with saying there is
\code{"nothing"} present for the ratio.
